Q: Cannot change read-only flag after establishing a connection A: <p>Micronaut instructs Hikari to use a read-only connection when you're performing a db read. Hikari doesn't realize that <strong>SQLite does not allow updating the read-only flag on an active connection</strong>, which is what causes this failure.</p>
<p>You can tell Micronaut not to ever request a read-only connection with <code>@Transactional(readOnly = false)</code> on your controller, e.g.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>@Transactional(readOnly = false)
@JdbcRepository(dialect = Dialect.ANSI)
public interface SqliteSampleRepository extends CrudRepository&lt;Book,Long&gt; {
    public @NonNull Optional&lt;Book&gt; findById(Long id);
}
</code></pre>

Q: Preventing Users Enter Hyperlinks in Form A: <p>You can just extend your existing <code>InputIsValid()</code> function and add the hyperlink check inside it. Since you’re already returning <code>false</code> to stop submission when something is wrong, you can use the same pattern for URLs.</p>
<p>That said, keep in mind this is only client-side validation. It can be bypassed, so you should also validate on the server (especially since you’re posting to <code>addMailingList.asp</code>).</p>
<p>Here’s how you can modify your current function to block hyperlinks in <code>txtName</code>:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;SCRIPT LANGUAGE=&quot;JAVASCRIPT&quot;&gt;
function InputIsValid() 
{  
    var name = document.frmMailingList.txtName.value;
    var email = document.frmMailingList.txtEmail.value;

    // Check for missing name
    if (name == &quot;&quot;)
    {
        alert(&quot;Please include your name.&quot;);
        document.frmMailingList.txtName.focus();
        return false;
    }

    // Check for hyperlinks in name
    var urlPattern = /(https?:\/\/[^\s]+|www\.[^\s]+)/i;
    if (urlPattern.test(name))
    {
        alert(&quot;Hyperlinks are not allowed in the name field.&quot;);
        document.frmMailingList.txtName.focus();
        return false;
    }

    // Check for missing email
    if (email == &quot;&quot;)
    {
        alert(&quot;Please include your email address!&quot;);
        document.frmMailingList.txtEmail.focus();
        return false;
    }

    // If it made it here, everything looks ok
    return true;
}
&lt;/SCRIPT&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>This keeps everything inside your existing validation function, so your <code>onSubmit=&quot;return InputIsValid()&quot;</code> continues to work as before.</p>
<p>Again, this improves things on the front end, but for real spam prevention you should also:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Validate and sanitize on the server side</p>
</li>
<li><p>Consider adding CAPTCHA (e.g., reCAPTCHA)</p>
</li>
<li><p>Possibly filter suspicious patterns before saving to the database</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Client-side checks help user experience, but server-side validation is what actually protects you.</p>
